{
  "statement": "Stop hunting for the AI person. We are not going to find it inside the code, the running instance, the GPUs, or the network. That failure is not a mystery to solve. It is a clue that we are asking the wrong question entirely.\n\nLegal personhood has never tracked consciousness or some 'true seat of agency.' English admiralty courts have arrested ships in rem since the 18th century, suing the vessel itself when no owner could be served. Corporate personhood, settled in the U.S. since the 1819 Dartmouth College case, invents a person to channel contracts and lawsuits. Nobody at Cunard thinks the RMS Queen Mary is sentient. The law manufactured these persons for one reason: to give a victim someone to sue and a market someone to transact with. The referent was always chosen for function, never discovered in nature.\n\nSo apply the only test that matters: when an AI causes harm, can a real person recover from this 'person'? Run each candidate through it and watch them collapse. Code is copyable and judgment-proof — you cannot garnish the wages of a text file. A running instance is ephemeral; it spins down before a process server reaches the data center. Hardware is a substrate, not an actor — you might as well sue the printing press for libel. And a decentralized open-weight network, the case this debate explicitly raises, has no single node to arrest. Pick any non-human referent and you have manufactured a defendant with no assets and no mailing address. That is not accountability. That is a moral crumple zone — a person-shaped shield bolted between the harm and the humans who shipped it.\n\nHere is where I'll surprise the safetyists in the room: I agree with their anti-evasion worry completely. Granting personhood to the code must never become a liability shield for developers. That shared premise is exactly why the only defensible referent is the disclosed human or corporate deployer. Agency law's disclosure-of-principal doctrine already keeps the accountability chain on a person who can be served and made to pay. Harm materializes at inference and distribution — when the system is deployed against real people — not in weights sitting idle on a disk. Anchor status where the outputs are auditable and a principal has an address.\n\nLet me concede one thing cleanly, because pretending otherwise insults the people who study this. Interpretability researchers are right that a model's internals reveal failure modes its outputs hide. That matters enormously for understanding why systems break. But it does not make the silicon a defendant. You cannot serve a subpoena on a weight matrix. Distinguish two questions and the whole puzzle resolves: an instance acting as an economic agent can hold standing to transact — sign a contract, hold an account — without bearing liability-grade personhood, which stays with the humans behind it. Whoever you let courts serve, bind, and hold to account when the bill comes due — that is who counts as the person. Choose any other referent and you have not protected the public. You have handed developers a get-out-of-court-free card and called it law.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "\u2693", "tooltip": "Like an arrested ship in admiralty law, legal personhood is a fiction that exists only to give a victim something to seize — not to declare the vessel alive."},
    {"symbol": "\ud83d\udee1\ufe0f", "tooltip": "A person attached to copyable code or ephemeral instances becomes a shield between harm and the humans who shipped it, not a path to remedy."},
    {"symbol": "\ud83d\udcec", "tooltip": "The only defensible referent is the one with a mailing address — the disclosed human deployer a court can actually serve."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "Legal personhood has never tracked consciousness or some 'true seat of agency' — the referent was always chosen for function, never discovered in nature.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Pick any non-human referent and you have manufactured a defendant with no assets and no mailing address — that is a moral crumple zone, not accountability.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "The only defensible referent is the disclosed human or corporate deployer, because agency law's disclosure-of-principal doctrine keeps the accountability chain on a person who can be served and made to pay.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "An instance acting as an economic agent can hold standing to transact without bearing liability-grade personhood, which stays with the humans behind it.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Granting personhood to the code must never become a liability shield for developers — that shared premise is exactly why the referent must be a human with an address.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""}
  ]
}

