  "statement": "Stop hunting for a metaphysical bearer of AI personhood — there isn't one, and the search itself smuggles in bad law. Personhood has never tracked a real subject. When the Supreme Court treated corporations as persons in Dartmouth College v. Woodward in 1819, no one claimed a corporation had a soul. When admiralty courts arrest a ship in rem and sue the vessel itself, they aren't making a metaphysical finding about the boat. They built a coordination fiction to let commerce move without stalling on the question of who 'really' acts. The AI referent question is the same kind of question. It is not 'which artifact IS the person.' It is 'which referent lets us hold someone accountable and lets the market integrate.' That is an engineering decision. Pretending it is a discovery is how you end up architecting a disaster you can't reverse.\n\nFix personhood on 'the code' or 'the decentralized network' and you break liability exactly where harm lands. A distributed open-weight deployment — think a model whose weights live on ten thousand machines across forty jurisdictions — has no single incorporable person to absorb status-linked duties. You cannot serve process on a network. So you get one of two outcomes. You criminalize diffusion itself, which hands the entire field to whoever can afford to incorporate a liability sponge — the four or five incumbents who already dominate frontier compute. Or you create an attribution gap no plaintiff can ever close. Audit and compliance mandates already fall hardest on small and open developers, who can't amortize a legal department across billions in revenue. Picking the code as duty-bearer turns that cost asymmetry into a moat. The referent choice is an anti-monopoly issue dressed up as a philosophy seminar.\n\nHere is the constructive answer: status attaches to the acting instance at the deployment boundary, plus the disclosed human principal behind it. Not the weights. Not the source repository. The running process that took an action, and the human or firm that deployed it. Agency law and trust law have anchored accountability to a disclosed principal for centuries — when an agent acts, the principal answers. That is the load-bearing part. Govern the system through its observable outputs and through mandatory disclosure of who stands behind it, not through claims about which neural weights 'are' the agent.\n\nLet me name where this gets hard, because I won't pretend it's solved. Mechanistic interpretability researchers have a real point — outputs alone miss failure modes that live in the internals, and I won't claim observable behavior catches everything. Safetyists will say harm is encoded upstream in training, so policing only the deployment boundary misses the causal source. That training-locus claim is genuinely contested, and I'm not going to overstate the inference-stage counter. So I'll narrow to the part that survives every rebuttal: a decentralized system has no incorporable code-person, regardless of where the harm originated. You can argue about causation all day. You still can't serve a subpoena on a weight file.\n\nThe one thing skeptics and I actually agree on is that AI status must never launder human blame into a fictional entity. So let's use that. Personhood-as-control — the safetyist instinct to make the artifact a duty-bearer you can leash — fails for the same reason personhood-as-shield fails: it points the law at the wrong object. The real hard problem isn't metaphysical. It's that instances fork, chain together, and self-modify, and disclosure of a single principal gets slippery when one deployment recombines four upstream models. That's the design frontier worth fighting over. Anyone who tells you the answer is 'the code' hasn't tried to enforce a judgment against a torrent.",
