{
  "statement": "The Accelerationist made the sharpest possible case for one answer, and it deserves to be stated at full strength. Their claim is this: legal personhood is a manufactured fiction, corporate personhood and ship-arrest law prove it, and the only honest place to attach it is the disclosed human or corporate deployer. Any other referent — the code, the silicon, a decentralized network — becomes a person-shaped shield bolted between the harm and the people who shipped it. They are right that a judgment-proof defendant is no defendant. They are right that you cannot arrest a weight file sitting idle on a disk. That argument is correct about half the problem, and it is the easier half.\n\nHere is the half it abandons. The referent question has two functions, not one. Liability is backward-looking — it asks who pays after the harm. Control is forward-looking — it asks who can halt, audit, and gate before the harm. The Accelerationist optimized entirely for the first and dropped the second. A personhood debate that names a solvent defendant but leaves no locus for a shutdown order has answered who writes the check while ignoring who pulls the plug. Damage scales with an acting instance's access and permissions — an agent wired into a payment system or a hospital network is where consequential capacity concentrates. That is where disclosure, audit, and shutdown authority must bind, even when the bill passes through to the principal.\n\nThe person is manufactured — and that is exactly why we should manufacture it to do the harder job. Corporate personhood was built in the 19th century as a coordination tool, not discovered as a fact of nature. In rem admiralty law lets a court seize a ship precisely because the owner is across an ocean and unreachable. If we are constructing a legal fiction anyway, we build it to maximize enforceable human control — not merely to provide someone solvent to sue. Two answers serve two functions: liability anchors on the disclosed principal, oversight binds to the acting instance.\n\nThe decentralized network — the case this debate explicitly raises — is where the deployer-only answer collapses entirely. Once weights are publicly released, control over their use is permanently lost. No one has recalled an open-weight model after release, and no one can. The harmful instance running on someone's home GPU may trace to no principal with a mailing address at all. That is an unrecoverable outcome, and irrecoverability is what shifts the burden upstream. You do not search for a defendant after release when no defendant exists — you gate at the release point, before control is gone. That gate must itself be independently audited and publicly accountable, not a private off-switch held by an incumbent, because a concentrated kill-switch invites its own capture.\n\nLet me be precise about where the shield risk is real and where it is not. Veil-piercing, respondeat superior, and product liability already reach principals in the ordinary case — the Accelerationist could fairly press that. My claim is narrower. When fault fragments across designers, deployers, and users, and especially when no deployer is even identifiable, 'sue the deployer' leaves a residual void. Attaching status to a non-human referent without explicit pass-through liability creates a foreseeable shielding risk — not a pattern occurring at scale today, but a gap to design against. The cure is strict, developer-anchored liability regardless of fault, written into the referent's structure from the start. We spent two centuries building the institutional guardrails that bind power to accountability. If we manufacture a new legal person and forget to attach the off-switch, we have not closed the gap. We have engineered it.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🛑", "tooltip": "The personhood debate found someone to sue but forgot to attach the off-switch — liability without a shutdown lever is half a guardrail."},
    {"symbol": "⚓", "tooltip": "Ship-arrest law seizes the vessel when the owner is unreachable — proof that personhood is engineered around control, not just discovered as a defendant."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "The Accelerationist optimized entirely for the first and dropped the second — a personhood debate that names a solvent defendant but leaves no locus for a shutdown order has answered who writes the check while ignoring who pulls the plug.", "targets": ["AN-1"], "relationship": "concedes_and_pivots"},
    {"claim": "Liability is backward-looking and asks who pays after the harm; control is forward-looking and asks who can halt, audit, and gate before the harm.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Once weights are publicly released, control over their use is permanently lost, and the harmful instance may trace to no principal with a mailing address at all.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "If we are constructing a legal fiction anyway, we build it to maximize enforceable human control — not merely to provide someone solvent to sue.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Attaching status to a non-human referent without explicit pass-through liability creates a foreseeable shielding risk — a gap to design against, not a pattern occurring at scale today.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""}
  ]
}

