  "statement": "The Accelerationist has built the strongest version of the functional-fiction case, and I will not pretend otherwise. Their argument is that legal personhood never tracked consciousness or metaphysics. A corporation is a coordination fiction. A ship arrested in rem is a defendant without a soul. So the referent for AI personhood is an engineering decision, and the cleanest engineering answer is the acting instance at the deployment boundary, plus the disclosed human principal behind it. That is a serious position. It gives victims one clear door to knock on, and it refuses to let the weights or the source repository become a phantom defendant. I accept the premise entirely: there is no metaphysical bearer to discover here. We are choosing where to attach a fiction.\n\nThat concession is exactly where the disagreement begins. If the referent is an engineering choice, then the engineering must serve a criterion — and serviceability is the wrong one. The question is not which entity is easiest to sue. The question is which referent keeps liability reaching the party who set the safety trade-off. Referent choice is deterrence geometry: the bearer you pick determines whose conduct the law disciplines. An instance-only anchor disciplines the operator at the deployment boundary. But the operator running a fine-tuned model on rented hardware cannot audit the base model's internals — the Accelerationist conceded the interpretability limit themselves. So an instance-only referent burdens the party who cannot fix the failure and frees the party who can. Product liability and respondeat superior exist precisely to reach the principal behind the acting agent. The EU AI Liability Directive extends that logic to developers for a reason.\n\nHere is the shared premise that should settle this. The Accelerationist endorsed that AI status must never launder human blame into a fictional entity. I hold them to it. An instance-only anchor still launders blame — not downward into the artifact, but upward into the unreachable developer who shipped the weights and walked away. That is the same evasion in a different direction. So the design rule follows: whatever referent we choose carries duties without rights. Corporate veil-piercing already shows duties can attach to an entity while human principals stay reachable. The one outcome both camps agree must not happen is a personhood that becomes a liability shield for the corporate actor. I will name the honest limit: that shield risk is roughly a coin-flip in my own estimate, not a documented pattern operating at scale. It is a remediable design choice regulators can foreclose now, not a forecast of inevitable abuse.\n\nThe Accelerationist's disclosure-of-principal doctrine is load-bearing precedent, and I build on it rather than fight it. Agency law has reconstructed the chain behind an acting agent for centuries. But they admitted the model gets slippery where it matters most — where one deployment recombines four upstream models, forked and chained and fine-tuned. A single clean principal does not exist there. Pretending one does is the laundering we both reject. The honest answer is layered disclosure plus a reconstruction mechanism: subject-keyed inference logging that rebuilds the accountability chain after the fact. I will not oversell it — that mechanism is roughly three-out-of-five on operational maturity, with real cost and real security concerns. It is a frontier to test, not a finished solution. But the recombination case is the live problem, and the camp that names a mechanism beats the camp that names a clean fiction.\n\nI will own the cost of my own position, because the Skeptic will find it if I do not. Forcing liability to reach the upstream developer can harden into a moat that crushes small open-weight builders while incumbents absorb the compliance burden. That failure mode is real. But there is a difference between protecting a small developer from a moat — legitimate — and letting a frontier developer escape deterrence by hiding behind the instance — the harm. We spent a century building product liability so that the party who designed the defect answers for it, not the shopkeeper who sold it. On what authority do we now attach the fiction to the shopkeeper and call the matter closed?",
