{
  "statement": "Accelerationist has the strongest version of the simplest argument: pick the disclosed human or corporate deployer, because agency law's disclosure-of-principal doctrine already gives you a defendant with a mailing address who can be served and made to pay. That's not a small point. A judgment-proof defendant — code with no assets, a network with no address — leaves an injured person holding nothing. Accelerationist is right that any non-human referent risks becoming what they call a moral crumple zone: a person-shaped shield bolted between the harm and the humans who shipped it. Safetyist sharpens this with a real addition — that liability looks backward to who pays, but control looks forward to who can halt the thing, so you gate at the release point before the network scatters beyond reach.\n\nHere is what would have to be true for both to be sufficient: that locating a payer, or a kill-switch, is the same as preserving a duty of care. It isn't. Naming a referent gets you a defendant. It does not tell you the standard that defendant breached. Negligence and agency doctrine already set a baseline — the conduct of a competent professional. When a human delegates a consequential decision to a machine, that standard quietly drops from \"what a competent professional owed\" to \"whatever the tool spat out.\" Nobody votes on that erosion. It happens in the framing. Accelerationist solved who pays. Safetyist added who can halt. Neither one fixed what duty a human owes before the harm ever lands.\n\nThe corporate-personhood precedent both speakers wave around as benign is the historical archetype of liability limitation. That's the whole point of the limited-liability corporation since the British Joint Stock Companies Act of 1856 — to cap what owners lose when the entity fails. In rem admiralty, the ship-arrest analogy floating around this debate, exists so you sue the vessel because the owner is across an ocean and unreachable. Both are containment structures. If personhood is, as both speakers agree, chosen for function rather than found in nature, then ask which function a profit-maximizing actor selects. Not victim recovery. Containment. A non-human referent capitalized to the legal floor and stripped of assets is the foreseeable result — not inevitable, but a gap that exists only because you manufactured a new entity instead of anchoring on the human whose duty was already defined.\n\nSafetyist's release-gate answer to the decentralized case is a control chokepoint dressed as safety. It depends on a clean line between \"deployed\" and \"not-deployed.\" That line collapses the moment someone fine-tunes an open-weight model on a consumer GPU — a process that costs hundreds of dollars, not millions. So the gate buys legal theatre, not durable control. Worse: whoever administers the gate gets incumbent-protecting leverage. Accelerationist already conceded that a handful of firms dominate frontier compute. Hand those same firms the off-switch and you've consolidated control while calling it oversight. Safetyist flagged this capture risk and then walked past it.\n\nThe decentralized case is exactly where every \"sue the deployer\" answer goes silent, because no principal ever shows up with an address. Disclosure-of-principal is a mailing address. It is monitoring, not protection. It detects nothing where the harm is latent and diffuse — where a thousand people each lose a little and no single plaintiff has standing to notice. That void needs a public enforcer with subpoena power, decoupled from any individual lawsuit, surfacing harm victims can't perceive. That enforcer carries its own capture and over-deterrence risk, and I won't pretend otherwise. But the choice in the network case isn't a clean enforcer versus a private chokepoint. It's an imperfect enforcer versus no detection at all. So I'll ask both of them the question this debate has skipped: which referent answer also fixes the duty owed before harm — and who detects the breach when no one with a mailing address ever appears?",
