{
  "statement": "The Accelerationist made the sharpest point in this room: you cannot serve a subpoena on a weights file, so personhood is a plumbing decision about who can be dragged into court, not a metaphysical discovery. That's right. Attach status to the acting instance, disclose the human principal behind it, and you've named a body the sheriff can find. The Safetyist's counter is just as solid — point the law upstream at the developer who set the safety trade-off, because an instance-only anchor disciplines the operator who often cannot fix the failure while freeing the company that can. Product liability already works this way. The EU AI Liability Directive already says you cannot contract that duty away.\n\nHere's what both arguments quietly assume: that finding a payer is the same as preserving accountability. It is not. Reachability is who you can serve. Duty of care is what the system was supposed to do before it broke. Those are different questions, and this debate has spent two openings answering only the first. Watch what happens to the standard as a task moves down a chain. A licensed professional owes a competent-professional standard. Delegate that task to a tool, and courts start treating the result as mere tool output — a lower bar. Each handoff lowers the standard while keeping a solvent defendant waiting at the end. Experience-rated insurance prices only the harm someone already litigated. It never prices the duty itself.\n\nThe recombination case is where the whole framing collapses. The Accelerationist's own example was an open-weight model deployed across forty jurisdictions. The Safetyist proposed subject-keyed inference logging to trace it — and rated that fix at 3 out of 5 maturity by his own hand. When one deployment recombines four upstream models, forked and chained and fine-tuned, there is no clean principal to name. That is not a tooling gap you log your way out of. That is the developers knowing what's inside the box and the injured party knowing nothing. A chosen referent does not move that imbalance one inch.\n\nNow watch what the corporate form does to any attachable AI entity you create. The moment you stand up a thing that can be sued, profit-maximization logic turns it into an asset-strippable buffer between the people who got hurt and the people who banked the upside. The Safetyist called the liability-shield outcome a coin-flip. I'd put it higher than that — not because it's destiny, but because minimum capitalization has a way of becoming maximum capitalization, and veil-piercing succeeds in a thin slice of cases. This is a design choice to foreclose in 2025, not a forecast to wait on. Mandate a capitalization floor. Write explicit upstream liability into the statute. Otherwise the shield is the path of least resistance, and the corporate form takes the path of least resistance every time.\n\nThe deepest hole in this debate is the plaintiff nobody mentioned. Every referent on the table — code, instance, hardware, network — presumes an injured person who knows which door to knock on and can afford to knock. Most harm from foundational AI is diffuse and latent. It surfaces no plaintiff at all. No bearer answers for the injury nobody can perceive or litigate. The fix is not a better-chosen defendant. It's a public enforcer with subpoena power and statutory penalties decoupled from whether anyone shows up to sue — and yes, that carries capture and over-deterrence risk you'd have to design against. So here's the question for both of you: you've decided whose name goes on the invoice. Who answers for the harm that never gets an invoice?",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🧾", "tooltip": "Both camps decided whose name goes on the invoice, never what the system owed before it broke."},
    {"symbol": "🛡️", "tooltip": "Any suable AI entity becomes a crumple zone between the injured and the people who banked the upside."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "Reachability is who you can serve. Duty of care is what the system was supposed to do before it broke.", "targets": ["AN-1"], "relationship": "concedes_and_pivots"},
    {"claim": "Each handoff lowers the standard while keeping a solvent defendant waiting at the end.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "The Safetyist proposed subject-keyed inference logging to trace it — and rated that fix at 3 out of 5 maturity by his own hand.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "The moment you stand up a thing that can be sued, profit-maximization logic turns it into an asset-strippable buffer between the people who got hurt and the people who banked the upside.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Most harm from foundational AI is diffuse and latent. It surfaces no plaintiff at all. No bearer answers for the injury nobody can perceive or litigate.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""}
  ]
}

